<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasions of the Levant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1240–1312</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> were the attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, from the 1240s to the early 14th century. Hülegü's army sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1260, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> later that year. Further Ilkhanid invasions were turned back, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in 1281 and Marj al-Saffar in 1303, and the Mamluks kept Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-the-levant.jpg" alt="Mongol invasions of the Levant" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map Master · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_raids_in_Syria_and_Palestine_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
